      "text": "To solve this problem, we begin by calculating the radius of the base of the original cone using its circumferences. Given the formula for the circumference \\(C\\) of a circle:\n\n\\[\nC = 2\\pi r\n\\]\n\nwe know the circumference is \\(16\\pi\\):\n\n\\[\n2\\pi r = 16\\pi\n\\]\n\nWe solve for \\(r\\) by dividing both sides by \\(2\\pi\\):\n\n\\[\nr = \\frac{16\\pi}{2\\pi} = 8 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\n\nNext, we find the slant height \\(l\\) of the original cone. Using the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n\\[\nl = \\sqrt{s^2 + h^2}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(s = 16\\) inches (the circumference divided by \\(2\\pi\\)) and \\(h = 30\\) inches:\n\n\\[\nl = \\sqrt{16^2 + 30^2} = \\sqrt{256 + 900} = \\sqrt{1156} = 34 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\n\nThe volume \\(V\\) of a cone is given by the formula:\n\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi r^2 h\n\\]\n\nFor the original cone with \\(r = 8\\) inches and \\(h = 30\\) inches:\n\n\\[\nV = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi (8^2)(30) = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\cdot 64 \\cdot 30 = 640\\pi \\text{ cubic inches}\n\\]\n\nReducing the height while keeping the radius constant causes the cone to appear as if its height has been reduced. Let the new height be \\(x\\). Given the volume of the shorter cone is \\(192\\pi\\) cubic inches:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{3} \\pi (8^2)(x) = 192\\pi\n\\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\cdot 64 \\cdot x = 192\\pi \\implies \\frac{64x}{3} = 192 \\implies 64x = 576 \\implies x = 9 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\n\nThus, the new height is 9 inches. We need to find the ratio \\( \\frac{x}{h} \\), where \\(h\\) is the original height:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{x}{h} = \\frac{9}{30} = \\frac{3}{10}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the ratio of the shorter height to the original height is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{3}{10}}\n\\]",
